01-intro 0.969  This is Culver's property intelligence prototype. It brings parcel research, public records,  and traceable residential calculations into one workspace. I'll walk through a real  Los Angeles property, then compare two hypothetical designs.
02-search 0.929  Let's search for 7661 Penfield Avenue.  The matching parcel opens with its address, assessor parcel number, zoning, and mapped buildings.  This property is zoned RS1.  Finding a parcel does not mean every calculation is supported.  The app keeps those limits visible.
03-evidence 0.939  The public map reading gives us a width of 60 feet,  a depth of 125 feet, and a derived area of 7,500 square feet.  These are machine interpretations, with explanations and source references.  This example reuses a completed reading.  On a new property, available results appear while extraction runs.
04-research 0.99  Property research brings together planning, permit history, engineering, and transit information.  Penfield has 11 permit and building records, including four downloadable documents.  Here is the recent certificate of occupancy. Engineering also returns  mapped easement features and nearby sewer records.  Each source stays visible so the user can inspect the underlying evidence.
05-assumptions 0.957  Now I'll switch to a detached ADU.  This is a hypothetical design exercise, not a finding that this particular proposal is  approved.  I'm documenting the assumptions about an existing dwelling, detached construction, kitchen facilities,  and coastal requirements.  The app distinguishes these user-supplied assumptions from source evidence.
06-height 0.953  With a proposed height of 19 feet, the selected rule branch shows a 16-foot limit.  The design exceeds that limit by 3 feet. Opening the explanation reveals the calculation,  assumptions, and cited provisions. That makes the answer inspectable,  instead of presenting an unexplained number.
07-compare 0.97  I'll save the 19-foot option, change the proposed height to 16 feet, and save a second version.  The comparison now shows one option exceeding the height limit and the other within it.  That result applies to this height check under the stated assumptions.  It is not an overall permit approval.
08-reopen 0.97  Saved scenarios retain their inputs, results, and evidence references.  Reopening the project brings the comparison back, so the reasoning can be reviewed later.  The workflow connects public source retrieval and model-assisted map reading to deterministic  calculations and saved decisions.  Missing evidence stays explicit.  The goal is faster, more explainable feasibility research.
